WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.201
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.201 --> 00:00:10.375
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:13.862
&gt;&gt; 2012 was a life changing year for me.

00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:20.146
In May I got pregnant, and then I found
out I was having a high risk pregnancy,

00:00:20.146 --> 00:00:25.000
requiring me to be on bed rest for
16 hours a day on my side.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.715
So to kill time and entertain myself,
I decided to go on amazon.com and

00:00:30.715 --> 00:00:36.160
buy all the highly rated parenting
books and started reading them.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.384
The more I read,

00:00:37.384 --> 00:00:42.646
the more confident I got about my
ability to take on this new challenge.

00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:46.118
I knew it was going to be hard,
but in typical Vadita fashion,

00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:50.006
I had figured out everything that
could potentially go wrong, and

00:00:50.006 --> 00:00:54.190
then come up with mitigation
strategies to address those challenges.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:02.000
So here I was ready to take this on,
and then came Zara.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.810
A very happy and healthy baby, 4.5 pounds,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:12.245
17.5 inches long with a vocal
capacity of 88 decibels.

00:01:12.245 --> 00:01:17.281
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Those first few moments were so

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:21.729
precious, the first few years not so much.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:25.936
I went back to taking a full time job,
which was very stressful.

00:01:25.936 --> 00:01:30.869
My husband Sindeep, was trying to start
a new business, so he also was working

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.085
really long hours, we hired a full
time nanny to take care of Zara.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:40.646
Zara was spending a lot of time with
the nanny, so not surprisingly,

00:01:40.646 --> 00:01:43.082
Zara got really attached to the nanny.

00:01:44.777 --> 00:01:47.656
And this really bothered me.

00:01:47.656 --> 00:01:52.840
But then I remembered in one of the books
that I had read, Sheryl Sandburg in

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.788
her book, Lean In, talked about being in
the exact same predicament, that I was in.

00:01:58.788 --> 00:02:03.922
So I was like hey, Sheryl went through
this, you're going through this,

00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:07.273
it's really no big deal,
just don't sweat it.

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:12.031
But that advice was really hard for
me to hold onto.

00:02:13.936 --> 00:02:20.290
The evenings got progressively harder,
and the weekends were even worse.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.460
Much of what I had read in those books was
just not working for me on a daily basis.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.876
And it didn't take me long to figure out
there was somebody else in the house

00:02:31.876 --> 00:02:33.716
who had it all figured out.

00:02:33.716 --> 00:02:37.857
Sindeep knew exactly what to say,
how to say it,

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:41.198
what to do, when to do it, how to do it.

00:02:41.198 --> 00:02:45.765
He essentially had the motherly
instinct that was supposed to be mine.

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:49.526
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was my precious gift for

00:02:49.526 --> 00:02:52.130
carrying our child for nine months.

00:02:54.128 --> 00:02:58.438
And for the first time in my life,
I was jealous of Sindeep.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.040
How could it be that I was so successful
at work, getting stellar performance

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.377
reviews, and at home,
I couldn't even put Legos together?

00:03:14.348 --> 00:03:17.224
It really frustrated me to see that, and

00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:21.560
I carried this frustration
into work everyday.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:27.635
So I distinctly remember that day in June
2015, I was basically running from meeting

00:03:27.635 --> 00:03:32.987
to meeting trying to cope with everything
that was being thrown at me that day.

00:03:32.987 --> 00:03:37.183
And it was Zara's wellness
appointment at 2:00 PM that day,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.101
and I didn't want to be late.

00:03:39.101 --> 00:03:43.252
But despite my best intentions I was late,
so I showed up,

00:03:43.252 --> 00:03:45.811
the appointment went really well.

00:03:45.811 --> 00:03:51.221
Zara was thriving as a toddler, and
then when the appointment was over

00:03:51.221 --> 00:03:56.827
Sindeep took Zara back to the car,
while I waited there, in the office.

00:03:56.827 --> 00:04:01.774
In this really tiny space,
probably ten feet by five feet waiting for

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:05.030
my pediatrician to print
her growth charts.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.800
So here I was standing there
very lost in my thoughts and

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.010
just staring at the ground.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:22.336
And she looked at me and
she said, So how's it going?

00:04:22.336 --> 00:04:26.665
I had no idea what it was
about those words, but

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:32.685
the next thing I knew, I was crying,
and this was no gentle cry.

00:04:32.685 --> 00:04:35.385
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This was really loud and

00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:40.531
ugly with lots of Kleenex,
smudged eyeliner, I was a mess.

00:04:40.531 --> 00:04:45.055
And I didn't know what it was, there was
nothing special about what she said, but

00:04:45.055 --> 00:04:48.175
it was as if she knew exactly
what was going on in my mind.

00:04:48.175 --> 00:04:54.620
She'd probably seen this before,
but for me, it was a first.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.337
And I was standing there
feeling really lost, and

00:04:58.337 --> 00:05:01.181
completely defeated by motherhood.

00:05:03.685 --> 00:05:07.060
It took me several days to
process what had occurred,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.392
and I think I finally figured it out.

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:16.336
&gt;From the day I got pregnant and
possibly even before I conceived,

00:05:16.336 --> 00:05:20.110
I had this image of this perfect mom.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.368
This mom who is on time for
all her appointments,

00:05:23.368 --> 00:05:28.150
her house looks really clean,
the sink is not piled with dirty dishes.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:33.051
The laundry is completely taken
care of tucked away in drawers,

00:05:33.051 --> 00:05:36.419
she organizes play dates for her daughter.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:39.395
She reads to her daughter probably
in more than two languages.

00:05:39.395 --> 00:05:44.418
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And she's on top of it, and

00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:50.780
I was miles away from achieving any
of this, on a consistent basis.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.080
And this really may have bothered me,
it made me frustrated and

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.980
angry, which made me
restless in my own skin.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.837
And my daughter could feel that,
which is why she was running away from me,

00:06:02.837 --> 00:06:05.546
making me feel worse and
this cycle just continued.

00:06:09.414 --> 00:06:13.602
So I have spent a lot of
time thinking about this and

00:06:13.602 --> 00:06:19.190
many Whiteboard sessions later,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:22.450
&gt;&gt; I think I finally cracked the code on

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:27.840
this internal dichotomy of a personality
trait that is so ingrained in me.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.500
So one one hand,
this continuous strive for perfection

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:41.490
leads to stress, anxiety,
it lowers my confidence levels.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.950
It makes me unsure of myself, and

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:49.015
in many ways it really increases my
desire to be accepted in this world,

00:06:49.015 --> 00:06:52.005
it's what made those first few
years with Zara really hard.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:59.530
But on the other hand, it's this
strive for perfection that results in

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:05.520
beautiful products, whether that is
at work or at home in the kitchen.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.314
You can ask my husband about
every meal that I cook,

00:07:09.314 --> 00:07:13.827
it is done in the absolute hard way,
there are no short cuts.

00:07:13.827 --> 00:07:17.914
But I think what it results in is
the perfect meal, or at least perfect for

00:07:17.914 --> 00:07:19.868
my status, and it makes me happy.

00:07:25.258 --> 00:07:31.587
And I really can not forget it is that
perfection that got me to the GSE.

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:43.247
This dichotomy is really tense.

00:07:44.368 --> 00:07:48.620
But this tension is beautiful,
it exists even today.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:54.880
What does not exist today though,
is this constant agony to try to fight it,

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:03.010
because what I realized is that
at my core, I'm a perfectionist.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.872
I just can not change that,

00:08:05.872 --> 00:08:11.352
what I can change is how and
when I apply it to my life.

00:08:16.368 --> 00:08:21.326
And because essentially what
I don't do anymore is try

00:08:21.326 --> 00:08:25.759
to apply this perfectionism
to relationships.

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:30.930
because relationships are not like
recipes, two tablespoons of love,

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.750
and three teaspoons of compassion
does not equal a happy baby.

00:08:42.302 --> 00:08:47.780
Letting my guard down to really be myself,

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:53.878
and who I am,
is what makes me the mom I want to be.

00:08:53.878 --> 00:09:00.834
And it is this imperfect mom
that Zara loves, thank you.

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:02.520
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.789
[MUSIC]

